# No Compatibility Layer

When changing or removing something, replace it cleanly. Do not leave a parallel old path alive.

## Do not add

- compatibility aliases for renamed methods, constants, files, or paths
- transitional layers that let old and new behavior coexist "for now"
- dead shims or wrappers kept only to avoid updating callers
- default-value fallbacks introduced to tolerate the absence of the new behavior

## Replace, then update callers

When you rename or restructure, update every reference in the same change and delete the old
definition. A reference that still points at the old path is a bug to fix, not a reason to keep a
shim.

## Why

Transitional layers accumulate. Each one adds a second code path that must be understood, tested, and
eventually removed — and "eventually" rarely comes. Clean replacement keeps exactly one correct path
and makes the change reviewable.

Removing the old path may surface broken references. That is the intended signal: fix them, do not
hide them behind an alias.
